# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
C={'01': 'The passages examined place marital and bridal language within Israel’s covenant and prophetic setting. Jeremiah describes the LORD as Israel’s husband, Isaiah calls Israel’s Maker her husband, Hosea promises renewed betrothal after judgment, and Isaiah connects Zion and Jerusalem with marriage and bridal imagery (Jeremiah 31:31–32; Isaiah 54:5; Hosea 2:19–20; Isaiah 62:4–5). Revelation 19 announces the marriage of the Lamb and states that His wife has made herself ready, while Revelation 21 explicitly introduces “the bride, the Lamb’s wife” and then shows John the holy Jerusalem, whose gates bear the names of Israel’s twelve tribes and whose foundations bear the names of the twelve apostles (Revelation 19:7–9; 21:9–14). Paul uses genuine marriage and espousal imagery concerning Christ and believers in Ephesians 5 and 2 Corinthians 11, but neither passage explicitly calls the Body of Christ the Bride. Paul instead identifies the Church as Christ’s Body, one new man, and one Body, and describes believers as blessed and seated in heavenly places in Christ (Ephesians 1:3, 22–23; 2:6, 14–16; 3:6; 4:4). Taken together, these passages retain bridal identity within Israel’s prophetic and Jerusalem-centered setting while identifying the Mystery company as Christ’s Body with the heavenly position and calling Paul describes.', '02': 'The gospel of the kingdom announces the kingdom promised in Israel’s prophetic Scriptures and proclaimed as at hand by John the Baptist, Jesus, and the Twelve. The prophets promised Israel a coming King and kingdom (Isaiah 9:6–7; Jeremiah 23:5–6; Daniel 2:44), and John, Jesus, and the Twelve announced that this kingdom was at hand, with the Twelve sent specifically to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 3:2; 4:17; 10:5–7). After the resurrection, the apostles still asked about the restoration of the kingdom to Israel (Acts 1:6), and Peter’s appeal in Acts 2–3 rests on what God had spoken through “all his holy prophets” (Acts 3:21). Paul, by contrast, explicitly calls his own message “the gospel of the grace of God” (Acts 20:24), and his reasoning from Israel’s Scriptures in the synagogues does not alter that commission. The passages associate the kingdom proclamation with the response God required of its hearers, including repentance and baptism, but they do not identify those responses as the content of the kingdom being announced. Matthew 24:14 further speaks of “this gospel of the kingdom” being preached before the end. Taken together, the passages present the gospel of the kingdom as the announcement of Israel’s promised King and kingdom, while Paul explicitly identifies his ministry with “the gospel of the grace of God.”', '03': 'The passages examined place the future prophetic Tribulation within Israel’s identified prophetic setting, while Paul describes believers as not appointed to the coming wrath and as gathered to Christ. Daniel fixes the seventy weeks upon Daniel’s people and holy city, Jeremiah calls the coming distress “the time of Jacob’s trouble,” and Matthew places the great Tribulation in the setting of Daniel’s abomination of desolation, Judaea, and the visible coming of the Son of Man (Daniel 9:24–27; Jeremiah 30:7; Matthew 24:15–30). Revelation likewise identifies the sealed 144,000 according to the tribes of Israel. Paul states that believers are “not appointed...to wrath,” describes Jesus as the One who delivers them “from the wrath to come,” and describes the dead in Christ rising and living believers being caught up with them to meet the Lord in the air (1 Thessalonians 1:10; 4:16–17; 5:9). First Corinthians 15:51–52 identifies the transformation of living believers as a mystery, while Philippians 3:20 identifies their citizenship as heavenly. Present Christian suffering is therefore distinguishable from the prophetic wrath described in the Tribulation passages. The interpretation of the “falling away” and the restrainer in 2 Thessalonians 2 remains disputed, and the preceding findings do not depend upon resolving those questions. Taken together, these passages place the Tribulation within Israel’s prophetic timetable while presenting the Body’s expectation as gathering to Christ, heavenly transformation, and deliverance from the coming wrath.', '04': 'The passages examined distinguish Israel and the Body of Christ by the names, covenants, promises, relationships, and expectations they explicitly assign to each. Peter describes the prophetic hope as that which God had “spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began” (Acts 3:21), while Paul describes the mystery as having been “kept secret since the world began” (Romans 16:25). Israel is identified as a nation with its origin in the promises to Abraham (Exodus 19:6; Romans 9:4–5), while Paul identifies the church as Christ’s Body, with Christ as its head (Ephesians 1:22–23). Israel’s covenants are named with the house of Israel and the house of Judah (Jeremiah 31:31), while Gentiles are described as formerly “strangers from the covenants of promise” and then “made nigh by the blood of Christ” (Ephesians 2:12–13). Israel’s passages speak of land, restoration, Jerusalem, and David’s throne; Paul describes believers as blessed “in heavenly places in Christ,” as displaying God’s wisdom through the church, and as awaiting their gathering to Christ and appearing with Him in glory (Ephesians 1:3; 3:10; 1 Thessalonians 4:17; Colossians 3:4). Taken together, these passages continue to identify Israel according to Israel’s stated national and covenantal promises and the church as Christ’s Body with the heavenly standing and hope Paul describes.', '05': 'The passages examined describe a consistent heavenly pattern for the Body of Christ. Paul identifies the church as Christ’s Body with Christ as its Head (Ephesians 1:22–23), describes believers as raised and seated together “in heavenly places in Christ Jesus” (Ephesians 2:6), and says they are blessed with “all spiritual blessings in heavenly places in Christ” (Ephesians 1:3). He identifies their citizenship as being in heaven, from which they await the Savior and the transformation of their bodies (Philippians 3:20–21), while Colossians describes their life as presently hidden with Christ and their future as appearing with Him in glory (Colossians 3:3–4). First Thessalonians 4:16–17 adds the resurrection of the dead in Christ, the catching up of living believers, and their enduring presence with the Lord. Ephesians further places the church’s spiritual conflict and the display of God’s manifold wisdom within the heavenly sphere (Ephesians 3:10; 6:12). These passages describe a present position as well as a future manifestation, and they do not reduce the heavenly calling merely to a future destination. Taken together, these passages present the Body’s heavenly calling as a Christ-centered identity, position, blessing, citizenship, hope, conflict, and purpose grounded in union with Christ.', '06': 'The passages examined define the Mystery by its revelatory history: it was “kept secret since the world began,” “hid in God,” and “hid from ages and from generations,” but was subsequently revealed (Romans 16:25; Ephesians 3:9; Colossians 1:26). Paul states that he received his message by revelation from Jesus Christ and that the Mystery was made known to him by revelation (Galatians 1:12; Ephesians 3:3), while Ephesians 3:5 also states that it was revealed by the Spirit to holy apostles and prophets. Ephesians identifies Jew and Gentile as fellow heirs and members of “the same body,” reconciled to God in one Body through the cross (Ephesians 2:14–16; 3:6), while Paul describes that Body as blessed and seated in heavenly places in Christ (Ephesians 1:3; 2:6). Colossians adds “Christ in you, the hope of glory,” and 1 Corinthians 15 identifies the transformation of living believers as a mystery (Colossians 1:27; 1 Corinthians 15:51–52). Ephesians 3:10 further states that through the church God’s manifold wisdom is made known to principalities and powers in heavenly places. These statements stand in contrast to the prophetic revelation Peter describes as that which God had spoken through His prophets since the world began (Acts 3:21). Taken together, these passages present the Mystery as a previously hidden purpose of God in Christ, revealed through Paul, concerning the one Body and its union with Christ, heavenly position, hope, and place in God’s revealed purpose.', '07': 'The passages examined describe the Day of the Lord within the prophetic Scriptures as a time of divine intervention marked by wrath, darkness, judgment, cosmic disturbance, and judgment involving Israel and the nations (Isaiah 13:6–13; Joel 2:1–11; Amos 5:18–20; Zephaniah 1:14–18). Zechariah 14 places the Lord’s visible intervention within that prophetic setting and describes His feet standing upon the Mount of Olives, while the larger prophetic record moves from judgment toward deliverance, restoration, and Messiah’s reign. Paul likewise says that the Day comes “as a thief in the night” and that sudden destruction comes upon “them,” but immediately distinguishes his readers as “brethren” who are “not in darkness” and are “not appointed...to wrath” (1 Thessalonians 5:2–9). In the preceding passage, Paul describes the dead in Christ rising and living believers being caught up with them to meet the Lord in the air and “ever be with the Lord” (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17). He also describes Jesus as the One who delivers believers “from the wrath to come” (1 Thessalonians 1:10). The interpretation of details in 2 Thessalonians 2, including the textual variation concerning “the day of Christ” or “the day of the Lord” and questions surrounding the chapter’s chronology, remains disputed; the findings above do not depend upon resolving those questions. Taken together, these passages present the Day of the Lord within Israel’s prophetic setting of judgment moving toward restoration, while Paul presents believers as awaiting their gathering to Christ and as not appointed to the wrath associated with the coming Day.', '08': 'The passages examined explicitly associate the Day of Christ with God’s continuing work toward completion, sincerity and blamelessness, confidence unto the end, and future rejoicing concerning faithful labor. Philippians 1:6 states that God will perform the good work He began until the Day of Jesus Christ; Philippians 1:10 directs believers toward sincerity and being without offence until the Day of Christ; Philippians 2:16 connects that Day with Paul’s rejoicing concerning his labor; and 1 Corinthians 1:8 speaks of confirmation unto the end and blamelessness in the Day of our Lord Jesus Christ. Other Pauline passages separately describe the Body’s future gathering to Christ, resurrection and transformation, appearance with Christ in glory, accountability before the Judgment Seat of Christ, and presentation of the church to Christ (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17; 1 Corinthians 15:51–52; Colossians 3:4; Romans 14:10; 2 Corinthians 5:10; Ephesians 5:27). These related doctrines contribute to the larger Pauline hope but are not individually called the Day of Christ in the passages that establish them. The wording of 2 Thessalonians 2:2 remains disputed between “day of Christ” and “day of the Lord,” and the conclusions above do not depend upon resolving that textual question. Taken together, these passages present the Day of Christ as a future Christ-centered horizon of completion, blamelessness, confidence, and rejoicing, within the larger Pauline hope of gathering, transformation, accountability, presentation, and glory with Christ.', '09': 'The passages examined present the remnant within Israel as evidence of God’s preserving faithfulness amid unbelief and judgment. In Elijah’s day, God preserved seven thousand in Israel who had not bowed to Baal (1 Kings 19:18), and the prophets repeatedly describe a remnant preserved within Israel through judgment (Isaiah 10:20–22; Jeremiah 23:3). Paul applies that pattern directly in Romans 11, where he asks whether God has cast away His people, answers “God forbid,” identifies himself as an Israelite, and states that “at this present time also there is a remnant according to the election of grace” (Romans 11:1–5). The remnant therefore remains a remnant of the people Paul continues to identify as Israel; its preservation does not create a new national identity. Romans 11 also distinguishes Israel and Gentiles in the olive-tree illustration and warns Gentiles against boasting, while Revelation 7 continues to identify the 144,000 according to the tribes of Israel. The remnant does not make God faithful; its preservation bears witness to the faithfulness of the God who has not cast away His people. Taken together, these passages present Israel’s remnant as a preserved believing portion within Israel through which judgment does not erase Israel’s identity or overturn God’s continuing purpose concerning the people He names as His own.', '10': 'The passages examined place Daniel’s seventy-week prophecy upon Daniel’s people and holy city, Israel and Jerusalem (Daniel 9:24). Daniel divides the prophecy so that sixty-nine weeks lead to Messiah, after which Messiah is cut off and the city and sanctuary are destroyed, while verse 27 describes a remaining final week (Daniel 9:25–27). Within that week, Daniel identifies a covenant confirmed with many, the continuation of sacrifice and offering, and a midpoint at which those sacrifices cease and desolation comes into view. Jesus later directs His hearers to Daniel’s abomination of desolation and states that great tribulation follows it, placing the Great Tribulation after the midpoint rather than making it synonymous with the entire seventieth week (Matthew 24:15–21). Scripture elsewhere uses corresponding three-and-one-half-year expressions that contribute to the prophetic description of this latter period, while Matthew 24 moves from the tribulation to the visible appearing of the Son of Man (Matthew 24:29–31). The prophecy consistently retains Israel and Jerusalem as its stated setting; the Body of Christ is not named as a subject of Daniel’s seventy weeks. Taken together, these passages present Daniel’s seventieth week as the remaining final period of the seventy-week prophecy determined upon Israel and Jerusalem, moving through its marked midpoint and Great Tribulation toward Messiah’s appearing and the fulfillment of God’s prophetic purpose concerning Israel.', '11': 'The passages examined distinguish the believing assemblies already present within Israel’s prophetic setting from the Church Paul identifies as Christ’s Body. At Pentecost, Peter addresses Israel, explains the Spirit’s outpouring from Joel, appeals to the Davidic promise, and proclaims Jesus as Israel’s risen Lord and Christ (Acts 2:14–36). The use of ekklesia does not by itself establish Body identity, since Scripture uses the term for different kinds of assemblies (Acts 7:38; 19:32, 39, 41). Paul, by contrast, identifies the church as Christ’s Body and describes the one Body as belonging to the Mystery that had previously been hidden and was made known through his distinctive stewardship (Ephesians 1:22–23; 3:2–6; Colossians 1:18, 26). Acts 9 records Paul’s historical calling by the risen Christ, while Galatians 1:11–17 establishes the independent revelatory source of his gospel and Ephesians 3:2–3 identifies the stewardship and Mystery made known to him. First Timothy 1:16 further describes Paul’s salvation in pattern-setting terms, although the verse does not explicitly call him the first member of the Body. Acts 9 itself does not state that “the Body began today”; that conclusion arises cumulatively from Paul’s calling and his later inspired explanation of the Mystery entrusted through him. Taken together, these passages place the historical beginning of the Church which is Christ’s Body with Paul’s Acts 9 calling and the beginning of the previously hidden Mystery Program, while Israel’s Prophecy Program continues alongside it through the Acts overlap.', '12': 'The passages examined identify the house of Israel and the house of Judah as the parties of the New Covenant and describe the covenant as God’s promised transformation and restoration of that people. Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises the law written within, restored relationship with God, knowledge of the LORD, and forgiveness, while Ezekiel 36:24–28 describes cleansing, a new heart and spirit, God’s Spirit within the restored people, regathering, and return to the land. These provisions do not originate Israel’s nationhood, land, King, throne, or Kingdom, since the Abrahamic and Davidic promises establishing those features precede the New Covenant (Genesis 12:1–3; 2 Samuel 7:12–16). Jesus identifies His blood with the New Covenant, establishing His sacrificial death as the redemptive basis of its fulfillment (Luke 22:20). Romans 11:26–29 continues to connect Israel’s future deliverance with covenant promise, and Romans 9:4 associates the covenants with Israel. Paul’s statement that he is an able minister of the New Covenant in 2 Corinthians 3:6 describes his ministry but does not identify the Body of Christ as the house of Israel, the house of Judah, or a named covenant party. Taken together, these passages present the New Covenant as God’s promised forgiveness, inward transformation, restored relationship, and spiritual restoration of Israel and Judah, secured through Christ’s blood and brought to fulfillment within God’s prophetic purpose for Israel.', '13': 'The passages examined present the olive-tree illustration within Paul’s discussion of Israel’s unbelief, Gentile blessing, and God’s continuing purpose for Israel. Romans 11 begins with Paul denying that God has cast away His people, and Paul immediately identifies himself as an Israelite while describing a remnant within Israel (Romans 11:1–5). In the illustration, Israel is represented by natural branches, some of which are broken off because of unbelief, while a wild olive branch is grafted in among them and becomes a partaker of the root and fatness of the olive tree (Romans 11:17–20). Paul continues to distinguish the wild branch from the natural branches after grafting and states that broken natural branches may be grafted into “their own olive tree” again (Romans 11:23–24). The illustration therefore changes the standing and participation of the branches without describing a change in their underlying identity. Romans 11:25–29 continues to call the nation Israel and grounds its future in God’s faithfulness and His relationship to the fathers. When Paul explains the Body of Christ elsewhere, he uses different language: believing Jew and Gentile are made one new man and reconciled to God in one Body in Christ (Ephesians 2:13–16). Taken together, these passages present the olive tree as an illustration of participation in Abrahamic-rooted prophetic blessing and standing, while the one Body describes the distinct Mystery identity God creates in Christ; grafting changes participation, not identity.', '14': 'The passages examined establish Acts 9 as a genuine beginning without presenting it as the immediate termination of what was already operating. Acts 1–8 remains centered upon Israel, Jerusalem, the Twelve, prophetic fulfillment, signs, repentance, restoration, and the promised Kingdom. In Acts 9, the risen Christ personally confronts Saul and identifies him as a chosen vessel who will bear His name before Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel (Acts 9:3–15). Paul later states that his gospel came through revelation of Jesus Christ rather than from human instruction and that he did not immediately go to the apostles before him (Galatians 1:11–17), while his later epistles identify the Mystery and stewardship made known through him. Yet Acts itself shows that Peter’s ministry continues after Saul’s calling: Peter heals Aeneas and raises Dorcas in the same chapter, remains prominent in Acts 10–12, and participates in the Jerusalem Council in Acts 15. Paul also repeatedly encounters Jewish audiences throughout his Acts ministry, demonstrating that Israel’s prophetic setting remains historically present after Acts 9. Acts 28 later records Paul’s application of Isaiah’s judicial-blinding language and his declaration that the salvation of God is sent to the Gentiles (Acts 28:25–28). Taken together, these passages establish Acts 9 as the historical beginning of Paul’s distinctive apostleship and the Mystery Program while Israel’s Prophecy Program continues, producing the concurrent operation of Prophecy and Mystery until the later Acts 28 suspension point.', '16': 'The passages examined locate the believer’s identity in what God has accomplished in Christ rather than in what the believer achieves through performance. Ephesians describes believers as blessed, chosen, accepted, redeemed, and sealed in relation to Christ (Ephesians 1:3–14), while Romans 6, Galatians 2:20, and Colossians 3:1–4 identify believers with Christ’s death and life and describe their present life as hidden with Christ in God. Second Corinthians 5:17 identifies the person in Christ as a new creation, while 1 Corinthians 12:12–27 describes believers as members of one Body formed by the Spirit, with Ephesians and Colossians identifying Christ as its Head. Paul further describes believers as complete in Christ, sealed by the Spirit, raised and seated together in heavenly places in Christ Jesus, and possessing heavenly citizenship (Colossians 2:10; Ephesians 1:13–14; 2:6; Philippians 3:20). These positional statements precede and ground Paul’s commands concerning the believer’s walk: Ephesians first establishes what God has accomplished and then calls believers to “walk worthy,” while Colossians grounds its commands in the realities already stated concerning life with Christ (Ephesians 4:1; Colossians 3:1–5). Taken together, these passages present Christian identity as a God-given standing in Christ that includes new creation, membership in one Body, acceptance, completeness, spiritual sealing, and heavenly position, from which the believer’s conduct is then called to proceed.', '17': 'The passages examined present the Abrahamic Covenant as a coordinated set of divine promises concerning Abraham, his covenant line, nationhood, land, and blessing extending to the nations. Genesis 12:1–7 promises that Abraham will become a great nation, that God will bless him, that blessing will extend to all families of the earth through him, and that the land will be given to his seed. Genesis 13, 15, and 17 progressively specify the land and its enduring connection with Abraham’s seed. In the ratification scene of Genesis 15, Abraham is placed in a deep sleep while the divine manifestation passes between the divided pieces, and the text states that the LORD made the covenant with Abraham (Genesis 15:12–18). Genesis 17 then distinguishes blessing upon Ishmael from the covenant specifically established with Isaac, and subsequent passages carry the promise through Isaac to Jacob, whom God names Israel (Genesis 17:18–21; 26:3–4; 28:13–14). The same Genesis passages repeatedly extend blessing to the nations without describing the nations as replacing the covenant line or becoming Israel. The Abrahamic promises also precede the Mosaic Covenant, and Leviticus 26:42–44 explicitly recalls God’s covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob even in the setting of Israel’s covenant judgment. Taken together, these passages present the Abrahamic Covenant as God’s pledged purpose concerning Abraham and the covenant line leading through Isaac and Jacob to Israel, including nation, land, seed, and an outward extension of blessing to the nations without erasing the identity of the covenant recipients.', '18': 'The passages examined present Peter and Paul as servants of the same Lord who were entrusted with distinguishable apostolic commissions. Jesus sends the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel with the Kingdom proclamation and promises them twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel (Matthew 10:5–7; 19:28). Peter’s early Acts ministry continues in that identified setting as he addresses Judaea, Israel, and the house of Israel and speaks to those he calls children of the prophets and of the covenant (Acts 2:14, 22, 36; 3:25). Paul is called separately by the risen Christ as a chosen vessel whose ministry will extend to Gentiles, kings, and Israel, and Paul states that his apostleship and gospel came through Jesus Christ rather than from human appointment or instruction (Acts 9:15; Galatians 1:1, 11–12). In Galatians 2, the Jerusalem leaders add nothing to Paul, distinguish what was committed to Paul from what was committed to Peter, recognize God’s activity in both ministries, and extend the right hand of fellowship (Galatians 2:6–9). Peter later refers to Paul as “our beloved brother,” demonstrating that distinction between their ministries does not itself require hostility (2 Peter 3:15–16). Taken together, these passages present Peter’s apostleship within Israel’s prophetic and Kingdom commission and Paul’s apostleship as a distinct commission received from the risen Christ and associated with the Mystery, while showing that recognition and fellowship between the apostles did not require their commissions to become identical.', '19': 'The passages examined show that the Jerusalem Council addressed a specific controversy over whether Gentile believers were required to be circumcised and commanded to keep the Law of Moses (Acts 15:1–5). Peter responds by recalling how God had already caused Gentiles to hear and believe, had given them the Holy Spirit, and had purified their hearts by faith; he therefore opposes placing the Mosaic yoke upon the Gentile disciples (Acts 15:7–11). Paul and Barnabas then report the work God had already accomplished among the Gentiles through their ministry (Acts 15:12). James appeals to the prophets and cites Amos concerning David’s fallen tabernacle and Gentiles called by the Lord’s name, showing that Gentile blessing agrees with Israel’s prophetic Scriptures without identifying Amos as a revelation of the previously hidden Body of Christ (Acts 15:13–18). James’s judgment is that the Gentiles turning to God should not be troubled with the disputed Mosaic requirements, and the resulting letter explicitly states that those demanding circumcision and Law keeping had received no such commandment from Jerusalem (Acts 15:19–29). Galatians 2:6–9 likewise records recognition of distinguishable responsibilities committed to Peter and Paul together with fellowship between their representatives. Taken together, these passages present the Jerusalem Council as recognition of God’s work among the Gentiles and a rejection of circumcision and Mosaic Law keeping as requirements for their standing before God, while preserving the distinguishable apostolic ministries already operating during the Acts overlap.', '20': 'The passages examined place Acts 2:38 within Peter’s Pentecostal address to Israel and the prophetic setting Peter himself establishes. Peter addresses “men of Judaea,” “men of Israel,” and “all the house of Israel,” explains the Spirit’s outpouring through Joel, appeals to Davidic prophecy, and identifies the crucified and risen Jesus as Lord and Christ (Acts 2:14–36). After Peter’s hearers are pricked in their hearts and ask what they should do, Peter commands them to repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins and promises the gift of the Holy Spirit (Acts 2:37–39). The passage therefore presents repentance and water baptism as commanded elements of Peter’s answer to the Israelite audience standing before him; it does not present physical water as the meritorious ground of forgiveness. Acts 3:19–21 continues the same apostolic setting by calling Israel to repentance in connection with sins being blotted out, times of refreshing, and the restitution of all things spoken through the prophets. Paul later describes a different baptism in connection with the Body, stating that believers are baptized by one Spirit into one Body and identifying “one baptism” within that corporate calling (1 Corinthians 12:13; Ephesians 4:4–5). Taken together, these passages present Acts 2:38 as Peter’s commanded response to Israel in the Pentecostal Prophecy and Kingdom setting, while Paul’s one-Body teaching identifies Spirit incorporation as the baptism associated with the Body of Christ.', '15': 'The passages examined set James 2 and Paul’s justification passages in different addresses. James writes to “the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad,” says that faith without works is dead, notes that the demons believe and tremble, cites Abraham’s offering of Isaac and Rahab’s receiving of the messengers, and concludes, “Ye see then how that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only” (James 1:1; 2:17, 19, 21–25). Paul states that “a man is justified by faith without the deeds of the law,” cites Abraham’s belief in Genesis 15, and excludes works from salvation by grace while stating that believers are created in Christ Jesus unto good works (Romans 3:28; 4:1–5; Ephesians 2:8–10). Neither letter names another Savior or another cross, and neither passage addresses the audience of the other. Taken together, these passages present works as the proof of living faith in the setting James addresses, and present works as excluded from the ground of justification in the gospel Paul preaches, with good works following in the believer’s walk.'}
